body:
Having played with this, I am becoming convinced that this should be in the navigation API, as it requires history indices that only the navigation API knows about (they come from the session history list), and in many ways it mirrors `navigation.activation` rather than `pagereveal`.

I'm thinking that the API should look something like this (we can of course talk about the names):

```js
navigation.addEventListener("documentchange", event => {
  // A `NavigationActivation`, pretty much identical to the one you'd get in the new document's `navigaiton.activation`
  event.activation

  // A `ViewTransition` or null
  event.viewTransition
});
```

Alternatively it can still be `pageconceal`, but with an actual activation info:
```js
window.addEventListener("pageconceal", event => {
  // A `NavigationActivation`
  event.activation

  // A `ViewTransition` or null
  event.viewTransition
});
```



WDYT @domenic ?

The two options I currently see for `pageconceal`:

```webidl
interface PageConcealEvent {
  readonly attribute ViewTransition? viewTransition;
  readonly attribute NavigationActivation activation;
}
```

```webidl
interface PageConcealEvent {
  readonly attribute ViewTransition? viewTransition;
  readonly attribute NavigationDestination destination;
  readonly attribute NavigationType navigationType;
}
```

I tend to like the first one because it's clear that a "navigation activation" (document change) is taking place.

With the former, to get the destination url:

```js
onpageconceal = e => {
  const url = e.activation.entry.url;
};
```
latter
```js
onpageconceal = e => {
  const url = e.destination.url;
};
```

The former also has `e.activation.from`, which might be useful?

> With the former, to get the destination url:
> 
> ```js
> onpageconceal = e => {
>   const url = e.activation.entry.url;
> };
> ```
> 
> latter
> 
> ```js
> onpageconceal = e => {
>   const url = e.destination.url;
> };
> ```
> 
> The former also has `e.activation.from`, which might be useful?

`e.activation.from` would be identical to `navigation.currentEntry`. The main ergonomic reason to use `NavigationActivation` is that you can have helper functions that take a `NavigationActivation` on concealing and revealing, rather than have to construct that yourself in a slightly different way. OTOH when you don't need that, `e.destination.url` is nicer than `e.activation.entry.url`.
